      "marks": 6,
      "answer": "$AB=8\\sqrt2$ (or $11.3$)",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$4x^{1/2}=x+3$, then $(x^{1/2})^2-4x^{1/2}+3=0$, or $16x=x^2+6x+9$. — M1. Eliminate $y$ and either treat as a quadratic in $x^{1/2}$ or square with a three-term right side.\n$x^{1/2}=1$ or $3$, or $x^2-10x+9=0$. — A1. If $x^{1/2}$ becomes $x$ in the first method, allow only M1 unless subsequently squared.\n$x=1$ or $9$. — A1.\n$y=4$ or $12$. — A1ft, following through their $x$ values. If solutions are found by trial substitution, B1 for the first coordinate and B3 for the second.\n$AB^2=(9-1)^2+(12-4)^2$. — M1.\n$AB=\\sqrt{128}=8\\sqrt2$ or equivalent, or $11.3$. — A1.\nTotal: 6",
